Sure! The latitude‑longitude pair you supplied is:

```
Latitude : 37.4224764
Longitude: -122.0842499
```

If you’d like to confirm this location or retrieve additional information (e.g., formatted address, place ID, etc.) using Google’s Geocoding API, you can perform a **reverse‑geocoding** request with the following URL pattern:

```
https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?latlng={LAT},{LNG}&key={YOUR_API_KEY}
```

Replace `{LAT}` and `{LNG}` with your coordinates and `{YOUR_API_KEY}` with a valid Google Maps API key that has the “Geocoding API” enabled.

### Example Request

```http
GET https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/geocode/json?latlng=37.4224764,-122.0842499&key=YOUR_API_KEY
```

### Sample Response (truncated)

```json
{
   "results" : [
      {
         "address_components" : [
            { ... },
            { ... }
         ],
         "formatted_address" : "1600 Amphitheatre Parkway, Mountain View, CA 94043, USA",
         "geometry" : {
            "location" : {
               "lat" : 37.4224764,
               "lng" : -122.0842499
            },
            ...
         },
         "place_id" : "ChIJ2eUgeAK6j4ARbn5u_wAGqWA",
         ...
      }
   ],
   "status" : "OK"
}
```

From the response you’ll see that the `geometry.location` field confirms the latitude and longitude you supplied, and you’ll also get a human‑readable `formatted_address`.

Feel free to replace the coordinates in the URL with any other latitude/longitude values you wish to look up.
